 ## Contracts
-- **B5 is native collection, not a new scatter worker.** Bounded real/max-plus/min-plus groups use the declared distinct-UID basis, or first ordinary bare assign when undeclared; predicate-destination groups remain refused; multi-axis and lowered-constant writers are admitted (slice B), and a boxed single such writer publishes the declared shape. `CheckedScheduledProgram.shapeObligations` enforces exact writer/basis extents, with existing inference errors allowed first. Both f64 and native f32 run; undeclared fallback is checked f32 only, not reference f64 evidence. `Eval.Plan.B5NativeTest` / `B5BoundaryTest` pin values, precision, payloads, unsized/rank errors and privacy. `route` explicitly refuses B5; the JAX codegen fixture requires actual prepared scatter nodes to refuse before emission. Current scope/evidence: `papers/semantics/additive_b5_record.md`.
+- **B5 is native collection, not a new scatter worker.** Bounded real/max-plus/min-plus groups use the declared distinct-UID basis, or first ordinary bare assign when undeclared; predicate-destination groups remain refused; multi-axis and lowered-constant writers are admitted (slice B), and a boxed single such writer publishes the declared shape. `CheckedScheduledProgram.shapeObligations` enforces exact writer/basis extents, with existing inference errors allowed first. Both f64 and native f32 run; undeclared fallback is checked f32 only, not reference f64 evidence. `Eval.Plan.B5NativeTest` / `B5BoundaryTest` pin values, precision, payloads, unsized/rank errors and privacy. `route` explicitly refuses B5; JAX `affineReference` renders and runs a group's writer scatters (see the JAX bullet below), while `einsumOnly` refuses them before emission (`b5ScatterRejectedBeforeEmission`). Current scope/evidence: `papers/semantics/additive_b5_record.md`.
 - **Evidence is the only way in.** Every worker takes private-constructor checked evidence; no raw-plan-plus-ops entry is exported. A checker reuses the local checkers' obligations instead of re-deriving them (`checkPlan` dispatches `checkAssign`/`checkScatter`/`checkScanPlan`/`checkPointwise`/`checkAxiswise`; `checkPlanBlock` reuses them plus `checkStepGraph`).
 - **One formula, two carriers; storage kind keeps them apart.** Binary64 and binary32 share each traversal through a private ops record (`ScalarKernelOps`, `NonlinScalarOps`) and differ only in the instance. Checked evidence records the `storageKind` it was checked for. **Every worker and adapter core checks that kind as its FIRST statement**, before arity or store work, and a mismatch is `storageKindMismatch`, never a conversion. The one ordering exception: the named `pack`/`unpack`/`pack32`/`unpack32` entries run `checkPreparedBindings` before reaching their core's guard (the runners' guard runs before bindings). Fixtures pin guard ORDER, not just existence. A new door needs its own guard; a new carrier follows the f32 shape (a `kind` parameter on each checker core, an ops instance, a graph worker beside `Dense32.lean`, a `StorageCarrier` instance and named boundary, and a guard at every door in `papers/f32_evalplan.md` §3.5).
 - **Current binary32 boundary.** Since F32-C, every step kind is admitted; nothing is refused by capability any more (§ "Still refused" in `f32c_evalplan.md` §1.2 for what remains refused by policy or by a different backend). Binary32 scan's independent oracle: `ScanDense32Test` Part D (`oracle32`). Binary32 scatter's independent oracle: `Scatter32OracleTest`.
@@ -56,7 +56,7 @@ Reachable from `import LeanNCD` (via `Adapter`, `Adapter32`, `Nonlin`, `RawStep`
 | Why was a source scatter rejected? | `lowerArith` (`DSL/Pipeline/Structural.lean`) reclassifies an affine/diagonal LHS into `Stmt.scatter`. Top level: constant and multi-axis placements are admitted (`lowerArith` rewrites a constant slot to a placement over a synthetic size-1 `#const<uid>` axis; a multi-axis row takes the exact-reach extent `LHSSlot.outExtent`); nonlinear placement and predicate destinations fail at capability tier; colliding writes are summed, never rejected. Since slice B constant/multi-axis writers are also collected in multi-writer groups and into a boxed single writer's declared box (`LHSSlot.collectable`; a raw `.affine (.const _)` still gives `multiWriterScatter`), and the surface spells them (`S[a + b]`, `S[=3]`); a scan-local surface constant slot is admitted since F3-b stage 1 (lowered to a unit-axis row; only a raw one in a hand-built `ScheduledProgram` is refused, `scatterOrAffineLhs` and `scanWriteRowNotAdmitted`), while a scan-local multi-axis slot stays refused (F3-b stage 2: `multiAxisScatterLhs`; the checked scan path cannot place a colliding multi-axis write yet, though the reference sums it since F3-a). In a scan, only positive one-axis affine placement with nonnegative bias in non-advancing state dims and identity nonlinearity is admitted; other failures are typed `ScanCompileError`s |
 | Differential checks | `test/Eval/Plan/DifferentialTest.lean`: `scanParityCheck` (checked plan vs legacy vs scan-free oracle), `scatterPrograms` (9 top-level cases) and `scanScatterPrograms` (7 cases, three-way). The 3,832-case scan-free and 17-case scan corpora are binary64 reference gates |
 | An oracle that is neither evaluator | `test/Eval/PropertyOracle/ScanUnroll.lean` — `independentRun` rewrites a scan into a scan-free program; calls no checked geometry or scan-worker helper. `test/Eval/Plan/Scatter32OracleTest.lean` — an independent binary32 top-level scatter oracle, pinning observed bits case by case |
-| Run checked plans against JAX | `experiments/jax_bridge/run-evalplan-affine.sh` (curated fixtures), `run-evalplan-affine-corpus.sh` (all `PropertyOracle.enumPrograms`), `run-evalplan-affine32.sh` (binary32 `affineReference`, `orderedReference32`) |
+| Run checked plans against JAX | `experiments/jax_bridge/run-evalplan-affine.sh` (curated fixtures), `run-evalplan-affine-corpus.sh` (all `PropertyOracle.enumPrograms`), `run-evalplan-affine32.sh` (binary32 `affineReference`, `orderedReference32`), `run-evalplan-scatter.sh` (scatter steps, ordered and `scatter_mode="fast"`, binary64 and binary32) |
 
 ## Pitfalls
 - **"Admitted by the checked backend" ≠ "runs on JAX" ≠ "runs in binary32".** Check each boundary separately (Contracts).
